        session_panel = f'<h3 id="session-history">Session-only privacy: a complete archive round trip.</h3><p>The serial repository now snapshots the complete archive into a private in-memory store, clears persistent content/search, and can restore saving. Every ordered full-payload hash, aggregate count and selected detail matches before, during and after the 100,000-record round trip.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Disable + clear flush</th><th>Enable + persist flush</th><th>Lifetime process peak RSS</th><th>Empty disk archive</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>{session["disable_flush_ms"]:.0f} ms</td><td>{session["enable_flush_ms"]:.0f} ms</td><td>{session["peak_rss_bytes"]/1024**2:.1f} MiB</td><td>{session["cleared_database_bytes"]/1024:.0f} KiB</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The full session snapshot uses more memory than ordinary paged disk history. Cleared disk space is reclaimed. Snapshot, purge and persistence failures retain pending work; an updated memory session stays readable after a disk transition fails. An IO probe detected no SQLite file opens during in-memory work and detected its real-file positive control.</p><p class="small muted">One exploratory transition on a shared Mac with primed OS caches; no percentiles or latency SLA. Barriers exclude migration, CLI spawn, full-hash verification, GUI and audio cleanup. Process peak includes the snapshot and verification. The original control failed with a 180-second child limit; a sample found per-row FTS secure-delete work in full clearing. Bulk clearing now resets the whole index transactionally, with trigger/content/index rollback tests. The failed helper did not retain the exact child exit reason, so no completed baseline timing or numeric speedup is claimed. Current source includes the paged UI; native history acceptance passed and package verification is a separate gate. <a href="../benchmarks/results/history-session.json">Complete transition and provenance</a> · <a href="../benchmarks/results/history-session-before-bulk-clear.json">Failed exploratory control</a>.</p>'
